ians be true?** Because Ethiopia has over 120 million people, 80+ ethnic groups, a landmass larger than France and Germany combined, and radically different regional conditions. A family in a drought-stricken pastoralist zone in the Afar region can be facing starvation at the same time that a family in Addis Ababa is shopping in a well-stocked supermarket. These aren’t contradictions; they’re different locations, different circumstances, different years. The problem is that the charity footage has been so relentlessly looped that it’s created an illusion: that everywhere in Ethiopia, at all times, the default condition is starvation. That’s the misleading part. It doesn’t mean the footage was staged—it means the *narrative frame* around it (that this is what Ethiopia *is*) is false. Your friend’s story is a genuine testimony of a hard life. It’s not “fake.” But if you then conclude “this is just how it is there” for every Ethiopian, you’re erasing the lives, kitchens, and dinner tables of over a hundred million people he doesn’t speak for. His suffering deserves compassion, but it doesn’t define the nation for everyone, any more than a friend who grew up hungry in a poor part of America defines the entire United States. I’m not asking you to disbelieve your friend or the footage. I’m asking you to see them for what they are: real pieces of a much bigger puzzle, not the whole picture. Does that resolve the contradiction?
